CAT = "melecWiring"; SEG = "WIR"
Q = [
("Three 4/0 THHN conductors (0.3237 sq in. each) go in EMT. At 40 percent fill, trade size 1 1/2 allows 0.814 sq in. and size 2 allows 1.342 sq in. What size is needed?",
 "Trade size 2", ["Trade size 1 1/2", "Trade size 1 1/4", "Trade size 2 1/2"],
 "The three conductors total 0.971 sq in., more than the 0.814 sq in. allowed in 1 1/2 EMT but within the 1.342 sq in. of trade size 2."),
("An angle pull box has a 3 in. conduit with two 2 in. conduits in the same row on one wall. What is the minimum distance to the opposite wall?",
 "22 in.", ["18 in.", "24 in.", "26 in."],
 "For angle pulls the distance is six times the largest raceway plus the sum of the others in the row: (6 x 3) + 2 + 2 = 22 in."),
("In an angle pull box, a 3 in. and a 2 in. raceway enclosing the same conductors enter adjacent walls. What is the minimum distance between their entries?",
 "18 in.", ["12 in.", "24 in.", "15 in."],
 "The distance between raceway entries enclosing the same conductor must be at least six times the trade size of the larger raceway: 6 x 3 = 18 in."),
("A 30.3 cu in. box has one receptacle and 12 AWG equipment grounding conductors. How many 12 AWG insulated circuit conductors (2.25 cu in. each) may enter?",
 "10", ["13", "11", "8"],
 "The receptacle counts as two 12 AWG volumes (4.50) and all the grounding conductors together as one (2.25), leaving 23.55 cu in.; 23.55 / 2.25 allows 10 conductors."),
("In box-fill calculations, each 6 AWG conductor counts as:",
 "5.00 cu in.", ["3.00 cu in.", "2.50 cu in.", "6.00 cu in."],
 "The box-fill volume table allows 5.00 cu in. for each 6 AWG conductor. 3.00 cu in. is the figure for 8 AWG and 2.50 cu in. for 10 AWG."),
("PVC conduit in trade size 3 must be supported at intervals not exceeding:",
 "6 ft", ["5 ft", "7 ft", "10 ft"],
 "The PVC support table gives 6 ft for trade sizes 2 1/2 and 3, 5 ft for 1 1/4 through 2, and 7 ft for 3 1/2 through 5."),
("PVC conduit expands about 4.06 in. per 100 ft for each 100°F change. About how much will a 200 ft run change for a 50°F swing?",
 "About 4.1 in.", ["About 8.1 in.", "About 2.0 in.", "About 1.0 in."],
 "Expansion scales with length and temperature change: 4.06 x (200 / 100) x (50 / 100) is about 4.1 in., so expansion fittings are needed."),
("Flexible metal conduit must be supported at intervals not exceeding:",
 "4 1/2 ft", ["3 1/2 ft", "6 ft", "10 ft"],
 "Flexible metal conduit must be secured within 12 in. of each box and at intervals not over 4 1/2 ft, with exceptions for short lengths at terminals."),
("Type MC cable must generally be supported at intervals not exceeding:",
 "6 ft", ["4 1/2 ft", "10 ft", "3 ft"],
 "Type MC cable must be secured within 12 in. of each box and at intervals not exceeding 6 ft, unless fished or used as a short luminaire whip."),
("Where conduit passes through a fire-rated wall, the opening around it must be:",
 "Firestopped", ["Left open for cooling", "Sealed with duct tape", "Packed with insulation"],
 "Article 300 requires openings around penetrations of fire-resistance-rated walls, floors and ceilings to be firestopped using approved methods so the rating is kept."),
("Which space may never contain wiring of any type?",
 "A duct for loose stock", ["A ceiling return plenum", "A hollow wall cavity", "A raised IT room floor"],
 "Article 300 prohibits wiring systems of any type in ducts used to transport dust, loose stock or flammable vapors. Plenums and raised floors allow listed wiring methods."),
("A raceway runs from a heated building into a cold storage room. It must be:",
 "Sealed to stop warm air", ["Painted on its exterior", "Larger than trade size 1", "Run without any fittings"],
 "Where a raceway passes between areas of very different temperature, it must be sealed to stop warm air reaching the cold part, where it would condense."),
("Direct-buried cable emerging from grade must be protected from the required cover depth up to at least:",
 "8 ft above grade", ["6 ft above grade", "10 ft above grade", "18 in. above grade"],
 "Article 300 requires direct-buried conductors and cables coming out of the ground to be protected by an enclosure or raceway from the minimum cover depth to at least 8 ft above finished grade."),
("Underground cables installed under a building must be:",
 "In raceway beyond its walls", ["Buried at least 6 ft below grade", "Of aluminum conductors", "Wrapped in warning tape"],
 "Article 300 requires underground cables under a building to be in a raceway that extends beyond the outside walls of the building."),
("What is the minimum cover for PVC conduit under a commercial parking lot?",
 "24 in.", ["18 in.", "12 in.", "30 in."],
 "The minimum cover table gives 24 in. for every wiring method in or under streets, roads, alleys, driveways and parking lots."),
("PVC conduit runs under a 4 in. exterior concrete slab with no vehicle traffic that extends 6 in. beyond it. What is the minimum cover?",
 "4 in.", ["6 in.", "12 in.", "18 in."],
 "Under a 4 in. exterior slab with no vehicle traffic extending at least 6 in. past the installation, nonmetallic raceway and rigid metal conduit need only 4 in.; direct-burial cable needs 18 in."),
("A 225 A panelboard is fed by a feeder protected at 250 A. The panelboard needs:",
 "Main device of 225 A max", ["Nothing more at all", "A 250 A main breaker", "Lugs rated for 300 A or more"],
 "Article 408 requires each panelboard to be protected by an overcurrent device rated no more than the panelboard, so a 250 A feeder device is too large on its own and a main of 225 A or less is needed."),
("In other than one- and two-family dwellings, a panelboard fed by a feeder must be marked with:",
 "Where its supply comes from", ["The installer's name", "Its voltage-drop percentage", "The service load calculation"],
 "Article 408 requires switchboards, switchgear and panelboards supplied by a feeder in other than one- and two-family dwellings to be marked with the device or equipment the supply originates from."),
("A switchboard with a top not fully enclosed is under a combustible ceiling. Without a noncombustible shield, what space is needed above it?",
 "3 ft", ["18 in.", "2 ft", "6 ft"],
 "Article 408 requires at least 3 ft between the top of a switchboard and a combustible ceiling, unless a noncombustible shield is installed."),
("At 480 V, with exposed live parts on both sides of the working space (Condition 3), what working-space depth is needed?",
 "4 ft", ["3 ft", "3 1/2 ft", "5 ft"],
 "The working-space table gives 4 ft for Condition 3 at 151 to 1000 V to ground. Condition 1 is 3 ft and Condition 2 is 3 1/2 ft."),
("The dedicated electrical space above a panelboard extends from the floor to how far above the equipment, or the structural ceiling if lower?",
 "6 ft", ["3 ft", "8 ft", "10 ft"],
 "Article 110 reserves the space equal to the width and depth of the equipment from the floor to 6 ft above it, or the structural ceiling if lower, for electrical use only."),
("Doors from a working space must open outward with listed panic hardware when they are within 25 ft and the equipment is rated at least:",
 "800 A", ["600 A", "1000 A", "1200 A"],
 "Where equipment rated 800 A or more is installed and a personnel door is within 25 ft of the working space, the door must open in the direction of egress with listed panic or fire exit hardware."),
("Single-conductor cables installed in a cable tray must generally be at least:",
 "1/0 AWG", ["4 AWG", "1 AWG", "2/0 AWG"],
 "Article 392 requires single-conductor cables in cable trays to be 1/0 AWG or larger, with smaller sizes allowed only in limited industrial cases."),
("Flexible cords must not be:",
 "Run through holes in walls", ["Used for pendant luminaires", "Used for portable lamps", "Used for appliance connections"],
 "Article 400 prohibits running flexible cords through holes in walls, structural ceilings, suspended ceilings or floors. Pendants and appliance connections are permitted uses."),
("EMT couplings and connectors installed in wet locations must be:",
 "Raintight", ["Set-screw type", "Painted after install", "Insulated throat type"],
 "Article 358 requires EMT couplings and connectors in wet locations to be listed for wet locations, that is, raintight."),
("A conduit body may contain splices only when it is:",
 "Marked with its volume", ["Larger than trade size 2", "Made of cast aluminum alloy", "Installed outdoors"],
 "Article 314 allows splices, taps or devices in a conduit body only if it is durably and legibly marked by the manufacturer with its volume."),
("At the cut end of Type AC cable, what must be installed between the conductors and the armor?",
 "An insulating bushing", ["A grounding locknut", "A duct seal plug", "A nylon cable tie wrap"],
 "Article 320 requires an insulating bushing or equivalent at the cut end of AC cable armor to protect the conductors from the sharp edge."),
("Type NM cable is permitted in buildings of which construction types?",
 "Types III, IV and V", ["Types I and II only", "Type I only", "All five types"],
 "Article 334 permits NM cable in one- and two-family and multifamily dwellings and in other structures of Types III, IV and V construction, except where prohibited."),
("NM cable passing through a factory-punched hole in a metal stud must be protected by:",
 "Listed grommets", ["A 1/16 in. steel plate", "Electrical tape wrap", "A short PVC sleeve"],
 "Article 300 requires listed bushings or listed grommets covering all metal edges where NM cable passes through holes in metal framing."),
("Trade size 1 EMT allows 0.346 sq in. at 40 percent fill, and 8 AWG THHN takes 0.0366 sq in. How many may be installed?",
 "9", ["23", "13", "8"],
 "0.346 / 0.0366 is about 9.45, and only whole conductors count, so 9 fit. Using the 60 percent nipple figure would give 13."),
("Service raceways entering through concentric knockouts with ring punches left in place must be bonded with:",
 "Bonding jumpers", ["Standard locknuts only", "Reducing washers only", "Plastic bushings"],
 "At service equipment, concentric or eccentric knockouts and reducing washers impair the bonding path, so Article 250 requires bonding jumpers around them."),
("In an industrial establishment, a busway reduced in ampacity may skip overcurrent protection at the reduction if the smaller section is no longer than:",
 "50 ft", ["25 ft", "10 ft", "100 ft"],
 "In industrial establishments only, protection may be omitted where the smaller busway is not over 50 ft long and rated at least one-third of the upstream device."),
("A surface-mounted cabinet in a wet location must be mounted with what airspace behind it?",
 "1/4 in.", ["1/2 in.", "1 in.", "1/8 in."],
 "Article 312 requires cabinets in damp or wet locations to be mounted with at least 1/4 in. of airspace between the enclosure and the wall."),
("Unused openings in boxes and cabinets, other than mounting holes, must be:",
 "Effectively closed", ["Left for future use", "Covered with tape", "Filled with duct seal"],
 "Article 110 requires unused openings to be closed to give protection substantially equivalent to the wall of the equipment."),
("In a wall of combustible material, outlet box edges must be:",
 "Flush or projecting", ["Set back up to 1/4 in.", "Set back up to 1/2 in.", "Recessed by 1/8 in."],
 "Article 314 lets boxes in noncombustible walls be set back up to 1/4 in., but in combustible walls they must be flush with or project from the finished surface."),
("Gaps in drywall around an outlet box must be repaired when they exceed:",
 "1/8 in.", ["1/4 in.", "1/16 in.", "3/8 in."],
 "Article 314 requires plaster, drywall or plasterboard surfaces that are broken or incomplete around boxes to be repaired so there are no gaps larger than 1/8 in."),
("The interior of a raceway installed outdoors above grade is considered:",
 "A wet location", ["A dry location", "A damp location", "A protected location"],
 "Article 300 treats the interior of raceways in wet locations above grade as a wet location, so the conductors must be listed for wet use."),
("Where moisture could reach live parts through an underground raceway entering a building, the raceway must be:",
 "Sealed or plugged", ["Sloped toward the building", "Larger than trade size 2", "Filled with grease"],
 "Article 300 requires underground conduits or raceways where moisture may contact live parts to be sealed or plugged at one or both ends."),
("Branch-circuit wiring above a fire-rated suspended ceiling may be supported by:",
 "Independent support wires", ["The ceiling grid's support wires", "The ceiling tiles", "The sprinkler piping"],
 "Article 300 does not allow ceiling grid support wires to support wiring, but independent support wires secured at both ends may be used, distinguishable from the grid wires in rated assemblies."),
("To prevent induction heating in steel raceways, all conductors of an AC circuit, including the neutral and grounding conductors, must be:",
 "Grouped in one raceway", ["Run in separate raceways", "Spaced 1 in. apart", "Twisted together every 10 ft"],
 "Article 300 requires all conductors of a circuit to be in the same metal raceway so their magnetic fields cancel and the raceway does not heat."),
]
